[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Identity and Access Management (IAM) in AWS, Azure, and GCP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Identity and Access Management (IAM) is the foundational security layer for any cloud environment. It answers two critical questions: Who is the user? and What are they allowed to do? This tutorial explores the architectural similarities and differences of IAM across the three major cloud providers: Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform (GCP)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of Cloud IAM"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Principals/Identities: Entities that can be authenticated and authorized to perform actions on cloud resources (e.g., users, service accounts, groups)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authentication (AuthN): The process of verifying the identity of a principal, typically using passwords, multi-factor authentication (MFA), or federation."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authorization (AuthZ): The process of determining what actions a verified principal is allowed to perform on specific resources."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"AWS IAM Architecture"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"AWS IAM is a global service that manages access to AWS services and resources securely. It relies heavily on JSON-based policies attached to users, groups, roles, or resources."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/*"
    }
  ]
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Azure RBAC and Microsoft Entra ID"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Microsoft Azure uses Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory) for identity management and Azure Role-Based Access Control (Azure RBAC) for authorization. Permissions are assigned using role definitions across management groups, subscriptions, resource groups, or resources."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"az role assignment create --assignee "user@example.com" --role "Storage Blob Data Reader" --scope "/subscriptions/12345-67890/resourceGroups/myResourceGroup""},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"GCP IAM: Roles and Service Accounts"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Google Cloud IAM lets administrators authorize who can take action on specific resources. GCP uses Primitive roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer), Predefined roles tailored to specific services, and Custom roles for granular control."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding my-project \
    --member="user:alice@example.com" \
    --role="roles/compute.instanceAdmin.v1""},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Multi-Cloud IAM Security"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement the Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP): Grant only the minimum permissions necessary for users and workloads to complete their tasks."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enforce Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA): Require MFA for all human users, especially those with privileged access."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid Long-Lived Credentials: Use IAM roles, workload identity federation, or short-term security tokens instead of hardcoded access keys."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Audit Regularly: Utilize tools like AWS IAM Access Analyzer, Azure Advisor, and GCP Recommender to monitor and clean up unused access."}]
